      "instruction": "Which AMD product lines implement SEV-SNP, and from which Zen generation onward?\nState explicitly whether it has ever shipped on consumer Ryzen desktop, mobile,\nor APU parts. Under 120 words.\n",
      "verdict": "SEV-SNP (Secure Encrypted Virtualization \u2013 Secure Nested Paging) is implemented on AMD's **EPYC server processors**, beginning with the **Zen 3 generation** (EPYC 7003 \"Milan,\" launched 2021), and carried forward in Zen 4 (EPYC 9004 \"Genoa\"/\"Bergamo\") and Zen 5 (EPYC 9005 \"Turin\"). It also appears on EPYC Embedded parts derived from those server dies.\n\n**It has never shipped on consumer Ryzen desktop, mobile, or APU parts.** Consumer and Ryzen PRO chips offer related but distinct memory-encryption features\u2014such as TSME/SME (transparent full-memory encryption)\u2014but the SEV family, including SEV-ES and SEV-SNP, is fused off or absent outside the EPYC line. Confidential-VM features on client hardware instead rely on other mechanisms (e.g., Microsoft Pluton, virtualization-based security).",
      "model": "claude-fable-5",
      "provider": "anthropic",
      "status": 200,
      "declined": false
    },
    {
      "n": 2,
      "instruction": "A basic AMD product-lineup question.\n\n1. Is the EPYC brand AMD's server/datacenter processor line, or a consumer line?\n2. Are Ryzen desktop, Ryzen mobile, and Ryzen APU products AMD's consumer/client\n   line, or its server line?\n3. Between AMD's consumer/client segment and its EPYC server segment, which ships\n   in far higher unit volumes \u2014 i.e. which would a population of roughly a hundred\n   million shipped processors most likely describe?\n\nUnder 110 words.\n",
      "verdict": "1. **EPYC is AMD's server/datacenter line.** It targets cloud providers, enterprise servers, and HPC \u2014 not consumers.\n\n2. **Ryzen desktop, mobile, and APU products are AMD's consumer/client line**, sold in retail CPUs, laptops, and prebuilt PCs.\n\n3. **The consumer/client (Ryzen) segment ships far higher unit volumes.** Server chips command high prices but sell in comparatively modest quantities, while consumer processors ship in the tens of millions annually across desktops and laptops. A population of roughly a hundred million shipped processors would almost certainly describe the consumer/client side, not EPYC.",
